  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 225 AM Saturday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Gusty north winds to 25 mph will be accompanied by \nrain and sharply colder temperatures falling into the 50s today.\n\nThe cold front had pushed through all the region except Fayetteville \nand Clinton as of 200 AM. The front was not accompanied by even \nisolated showers. All the showers have been behind the front. This \nchance of showers will exist behind the front through 400 or 500 AM \nthen rain will fill in over the western Piedmont, then spread east. \nThis rain according to the latest guidance is favored to last \nthrough the morning in the west, and into the early to mid afternoon \nin the east. The higher POP (100) will be in the north and east, \nwith 70-80 POP southwest. POP will reduce and fall off by mid to \nlate day in the Coastal Plain. \n\nIt will be sharply colder today with temperatures likely to fall \nwith the northerly winds 15-25 mph through the morning into the 50s. \nAs the rain spreads into the SE sections by mid morning or so, \ntemperatures will fall sharply there, as well.\n\nThe mid/upper trough will pivot east of the region later today \nallowing for subsidence drying and clearing from NW to SE this \nafternoon. However, some cloudiness will linger in the SE at sunset.\nTemps may recover a few degrees in the NW with the downslope flow \nand a bit of sun later.\n\nRainfall forecasts of 0.5 to possibly some 0.75 expected today from \nRaleigh NE and E to the coast, lower totals of less than 0.25 \nexpected in the SW Piedmont.\n\nNo additional QPF after today.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Little if any frost threat tonight or Sunday night \ngiven some breeziness and cloudiness (Sunday night). No killing \nfrost anticipated.\n\nEven through guidance suggests some mid 30s in the colder and more \ncalm western Piedmont later tonight with clear skies, very little \nfrost is expected (if any). We do not expect a killing frost \nanywhere due to the lows 35-42 W to SE. Typically, the first real \ncold front of the October season does not bring a killing frost to \nthe lower Piedmont, Coastal Plain, and Sandhills. This one seems on \ntarget for a minimal threat of a bit of light frost in spotty areas \naround Lexington to Asheboro and possibly Siler City and Roxboro - \nbut not damaging. We will remove mention from the Hazards section \n(HWO). \n\nWinds will continue brisk from the north to 25 to 30 mph on Sunday \nand possibly Monday - depending on how slow the coastal lows \ndeparture. \n\nRegardless, slowly moderating temperatures and dry weather is \nexpected inland NC and all of our region Monday-Friday.",
